WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.960
Before we get stuck into Blueprints, let's discuss some terminology that might be useful

2
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.000
to help you get familiar with the concepts of Blueprints.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.920
So in Unreal Engine, if you've not used any previous versions before, you can see

4
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:25.280
that a class is the highest type of template for anything that contains information.

5
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.559
So everything in Unreal is contained within a class.

6
00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:31.960
Within a class, you can get objects.

7
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.640
This is the basis of all of the UE classes.

8
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.880
And it's a self-contained set of code, data, variables, essentially anything that holds

9
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.000
information such as functions, macros, these kind of things.

10
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.020
And then we have Actors in Unreal Engine.

11
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.719
And an Actor is a child class of an object.

12
00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:54.200
And we'll go through a series of flow diagrams just to show you this flow of information.

13
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.640
But essentially Actors are all Unreal Engine specific.

14
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.500
They're things that define a special kind of object that can be placed in a level,

15
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.539
so they have X, Y, and Z coordinates.

16
00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:07.060
They have properties that they can house.

17
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:11.379
You can have different Actor types like lights, cameras, meshes.

18
00:01:11.379 --> 00:01:15.700
So if we just think about it from a kind of flow-based perspective, we start with

19
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:24.040
a class, we move to an object, and then an Actor is then what you would place inside the Editor.

20
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:29.420
So to give you a better idea about that description, if we think about an object,

21
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.579
Actors hold information, but they don't necessarily exist anywhere in the world.

22
00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:39.819
They have this information that we spoke about before, they have the data that they

23
00:01:39.819 --> 00:01:45.459
can execute, but they don't actually, they're not housed anywhere.

24
00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:51.340
Whereas an Actor in Unreal Engine, the Actor thinks because it is based on an object,

25
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:57.180
so it has the properties of an object, and so an Actor can therefore say okay I exist

26
00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:03.459
in the world, whether that be an X, Y, or Z coordinate in the Editor, through a camera,

27
00:02:03.459 --> 00:02:07.739
a light, any of these Actor properties that we mentioned before.

28
00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:13.780
And this is Unreal Engine concept for Unreal Engine 5, and it's been used in previous

29
00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.439
Engines as well.

30
00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:23.240
So if we think about the C++ terminology, parent classes in Unreal Engine, specific

31
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.680
classes that a child can inherit from.

32
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.039
So when we're talking about this flow of information, we're talking about having these

33
00:02:30.039 --> 00:02:35.479
parent classes that hold the majority of the information, well maybe not the majority

34
00:02:35.479 --> 00:02:41.879
of information, but all the core functionality that you would need within any child classes.

35
00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:47.400
So it means that you don't have to rewrite code, you don't have to duplicate, a whole

36
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.580
bunch of information for things that you want had to have similar setups.

37
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:57.020
So in Unreal Engine, the main thing to take away here is that each parent class can

38
00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.460
have many child classes.

39
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:06.379
And a child class inherits from this parent class, so you could refer to it as a subclass.

40
00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:10.900
So when we're talking about the light example from the previous slide, when we're

41
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:16.139
talking about Actors in the level, parent class in this example would be the Point

42
00:03:16.139 --> 00:03:18.129
Light Actor.

43
00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:23.449
And therefore a Light Actor class is a child of the actor class.

44
00:03:23.449 --> 00:03:26.090
And each child can only have a single parent class.

45
00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:32.849
So you can't have this larger parent class and then create a child class from it.

46
00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:37.270
And that child class has then multiple parents.

47
00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:42.250
The easiest way to think about it is that each parent class can create many child

48
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:48.530
classes, so there's a flow, a downward flow of information from the main parent class.

49
00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.330
And the child class can only have a single parent.

50
00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.669
And what a parent class already contains is passed to the child.

51
00:03:55.669 --> 00:04:00.909
So you can override this functionality inside the child class from the parent class, but

52
00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:07.569
you can create information in the child class that then propagates up to the parent.

53
00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:11.300
It's always a downward flow of information.

54
00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.919
To give you an idea about this in a graph, we have the parent at the top, we have

55
00:04:14.919 --> 00:04:20.199
a child class below, and you can still create these multiple child actors from the parent

56
00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:24.000
class, however it doesn't work in the opposite direction.

57
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.360
And then to give you a deeper understanding more in the context of when we're making

58
00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:34.160
games in Unreal Engine, all blueprint classes have a parent up until the object class,

59
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.459
which is a U object, which essentially stands for Unreal Object.

60
00:04:38.459 --> 00:04:40.459
This is the origin class.

61
00:04:40.459 --> 00:04:45.100
When you make a new blueprint, you must derive a child from an existing class.

62
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.939
So whenever you have a child class, it will always come from a previous class.

63
00:04:50.939 --> 00:04:53.579
Object is the highest level and everything else comes from there.

64
00:04:53.579 --> 00:04:58.259
So if we're talking about a character running through a scene, for example, you

65
00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:03.019
can see the flow from, let's go from right to left here.

66
00:05:03.019 --> 00:05:05.259
You start with this character classes.

67
00:05:05.259 --> 00:05:07.899
You think, where did the character get its properties from?

68
00:05:07.899 --> 00:05:11.899
Well in Unreal Engine, you have something called a Pawn, which has a bunch of these

69
00:05:11.899 --> 00:05:14.379
properties that the child can inherit.

70
00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:17.180
So with Pawn, you have Actor, which we referred to before.

71
00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:22.779
The actor belongs in this 3D space, and the actor came from this larger object class.

72
00:05:22.779 --> 00:05:27.860
And so that's essentially the class structure of how information flows, and it will help

73
00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.550
give context to when you are creating your blueprints.

74
00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:38.709
When we're talking about inheritance, in almost all aspects of its design, including

75
00:05:38.709 --> 00:05:42.709
UE assets, there is some form of inheritance.

76
00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:47.990
So using that previous example before from the character, the character inherited a whole

77
00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:55.029
bunch of information that it prevents a duplication of information, and it just creates the communication

78
00:05:55.029 --> 00:05:57.629
lines very clear in terms of where this came from.

79
00:05:57.629 --> 00:06:02.949
So when we're opening blueprints up, say we open the character blueprint, for example,

80
00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:08.430
still using this character example, you can see in the character blueprint that you can

81
00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:13.269
go up through to the Pawn class, up through the Actor class, and into the Object class.

82
00:06:13.269 --> 00:06:16.110
And we'll show this in more detail as we get into the course.
